WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.900
I'm going to tell you
two things in this talk.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.910
First, how you can
build accessible

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.270
lessons with The Carpentries Workbench.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:17.540
And second, how we used R to
build The Carpentries workbench,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.060
which allowed us to
transform our lessons

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.970
from looking like this
to looking something

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.790
a bit more like this.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:29.810
And I have a lot of people that
I need to thank for this talk,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.240
including the RStudio
diversity scholars

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.960
program, which brought myself
and Dr. SherAaron, soon to be Dr. SherAaron

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.840
Hurt to this conference.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.950
But I don't really have
the time to go over this.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.390
And what you need to
know about this talk

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.050
is that you need to know who
The Carpentries are in order

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.210
to appreciate it and
in order to understand

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.220
who The Carpentries are.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.678
I'm going to tell
you a little bit,

00:00:52.678 --> 00:00:55.300
a little story about myself.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.610
This is not a recent photo.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.360
This was from 2014.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.360
I was teaching my very
first conference workshop

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.269
in population genetics
and are giving it

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:06.310
to plant pathologists.
If you're wondering

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.770
what population
genetics in R is,

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.190
think 23andMe but for fungi.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.480
So in this workshop I wrote
the, I wrote the materials

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.000
all in R Markdown.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.570
I forced my, I forced
my advisor to use Git

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.880
and ended up fixing a
lot of merge conflicts.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.590
And the process of building
this was very painful.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.150
It was very much close your
eyes and hope it works.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:39.010
I did end up building the
website, but that version of me

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.030
knew quite a lot about
population genetics,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.340
knew quite a lot
about plant pathology,

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.950
didn't know very much about
inclusive teaching, lesson

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.910
design, or live coding.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.520
And this workshop, it
was actually successful.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.600
We ran it for two years,
got really good reviews.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.390
People liked it, but something
was really bothering me.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.490
Something was eating
me on the inside.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:08.360
I didn't actually know
what people learned.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.850
I knew they learned
something.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.100
They had their computers open.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.860
They were following along.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.720
But I don't know
what they learned.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.890
And here's the thing.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.750
My advisor had no
way of supporting me

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.930
in that he was a
government scientist.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.230
You know, like he hadn't
taught a course in years.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:28.550
And so if I had a way to
get the support I needed,

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.490
such as maybe a template that
could have that could have

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:36.680
given me the narrative
elements and, and elements

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.900
for formative
assessment, I would

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.910
have been able to
succeed in understanding

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.060
what my learners
learned and then improve

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.010
that lesson over time.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.730
And I know now that The Carpentries
provides that support

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.680
and gives a safe space
for improving my teaching.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.790
And The Carpentries is all about
spreading data science skills

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.440
to research researchers around
the globe to really democratize

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.420
access to data.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.530
And it does this by fostering
communities of practice.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.320
And these are volunteers
who are trained

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.270
in evidence based
teaching principles

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.520
from all around the world.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:22.550
And we all come together through
shared core values in that we

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.160
are motivated, in that
we are always learning

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.440
and we're inclusive of all.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.070
In practice, we act openly,
we empower one another,

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.140
and we value all
contributions, which

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:37.490
allows us to achieve our
goals of championing people

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:42.140
first, access for all, community
collaboration, and strength

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.790
through diversity.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.520
And we know that
it works because we

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:52.490
have led over 3,500 workshops
in the past 10 years

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.540
to over 60 countries.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:04:02.720
3900 instructors, 45 lessons
to over 80,000 novice learners

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.980
and over 90 member institutions.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.810
I know that's a lot of numbers.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.660
But the key to this.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.570
The reason why this works is
because our instructors never

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.019
teach alone in a
Carpentries workshop.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.084
If you are an instructor,
if you're a new instructor,

00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:20.459
you're always
paired with someone

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.019
who's taught this before.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:26.280
And this is always possible
because of the efforts

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.790
of our director of
workshops, SherAaron Hurt,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.550
who allows you, who
gives you the opportunity

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.350
to teach these workshops.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.690
And these workshops
would not be possible

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.230
without our open source and
open license materials that

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.210
are available on GitHub.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.160
And it's these
materials that I'm

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.730
going to talk about for
the rest of the talk,

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.390
because these have
a long history.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:54.000
They were originally written
in HTML, just straight HTML,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.640
really difficult to write
if you're really tired or.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.890
Yeah eventually
they switched over

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:06.870
to jekyll, which is GitHub's
default site generator.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.690
And I'm going to ask for a
little audience participation

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:14.230
here, so I'm going to
ask you three questions.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:19.780
If the answer is yes, I want
you to clap twice with me.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:23.050
So first question just
to get a baseline.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.970
If you have ever used Jekyll.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.860
Clap twice.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.210
OK, good.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.840
If you have ever uttered a
curse word while using Jekyll.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.990
Clap twice.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.230
That was about the same.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:40.150
OK if you have ever wanted
to set your laptop on fire

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.690
while using Jekyll,
please clap twice.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.590
OK so, yeah, Jekyll's
kind of hard to use.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:52.030
And the thing is,
it's been shown

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.210
as a barrier when you're just
trying to build the lesson.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:58.420
When you're trying
to work on a lesson,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:05:59.920
you just want to fix a typo.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.080
You want to update an image.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.780
And if you find this.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.490
You really want to
burn it all down?

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.640
It's not easy to use.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.650
And the thing is, it wasn't
living up to our values.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.580
This really did not
put people first.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.600
This was putting
the machines first.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.650
And so I was hired in 2020 to
reimagine this infrastructure

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.860
because it was clear it was
not living up to our values.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.680
And what I realized
is that we really

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.730
need to separate the
tools from the content.

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.340
Anything everybody should
worry about is just

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.910
writing, being able to
write Markdown or R Markdown

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.010
and get a website
out of it and not

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.910
having to sift through
all the output that's

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.640
contained in the
same folder or having

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.150
to worry about tools breaking.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:56.250
And so this is how I created
The Carpentries Workbench.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.490
And this is a series
of packages that

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.550
avoids having a lesson
being wrapped around a blog

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:03.690
infrastructure.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.210
And it keeps the tools
and output separate.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.760
And it's based on the
philosophy of package

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.150
down, work where you are.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:16.733
And this, it consists
of three packages.

00:07:16.733 --> 00:07:18.900
The first package that
you're going to interact with

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.220
is sandpaper.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.150
This is what
orchestrates everything.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:25.770
The only command you really
need to know is sandpaper serve.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.420
The next package
is on the inside.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.060
And you don't interact with it.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:32.560
So often, but you do with it.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.150
You do sandpaper, uses
it in the back end

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.850
to help you validate
your markdown,

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.040
to make sure that
it actually is going

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.240
to do what you want it to do.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.850
And then coming in
at the end is varnish

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.660
that allows you, that gives you
the styling of your lessons

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.620
to make sure that your lessons
are accessible and beautiful.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.540
And all of this
was designed what

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.420
I like to call with, what I
like to call core values driven

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:59.620
design.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.770
And so I'm going to talk
about a few of our core values

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.680
and how the design
elements fit in with them.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.250
There are a lot more, but I
don't have that much time.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:10.760
So the first thing
I'm going to I'm

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.760
going to start with is
championing people first,

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.720
because really, individuals
who make up our community

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.960
are the most important
part of our organization

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.490
and they are our
strongest resource.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:26.680
So it only makes sense that when
I was looking for a static site

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.090
generator, something to generate
our websites, with that I

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:37.039
would choose something that had
the most on ramps and really.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.559
R is the best resource.

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:42.440
It makes the most sense.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:47.330
You saw it in Iliana's talk and
you see it here and this tweet

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.730
from Yanina Bellini,
and she says

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:51.410
that some people
laugh when I tell them

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:55.370
that R's best feature as
a language is its community.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.920
But I'm very serious,
especially RLadies

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.150
has been such a positive agent
of change for so many people.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.450
And that's one of the
reasons why y'all are here.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.010
Like, we are a good
community here.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.970
We know how to make
people feel included.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:14.180
And so it only makes sense.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.250
Another way that we're
championing people

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.790
first is that we virtually
increased the number

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.660
of instructors in the room.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.320
When you're teaching a lesson
and I'm going to explain what

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:24.990
I mean by that.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.450
So you have your lesson.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.520
It has code examples,
and you want

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:33.470
to teach someone how to add
a column to a dataframe.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:38.210
So you are working in RStudio
and you show them the code,

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:39.800
you run the code,
and then you have

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.690
output that looks like this.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:44.090
You can't really tell what
happened because there are so

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:45.480
many columns in the dataframe.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.940
Everything ran off the edge.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:48.790
If you were.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:53.747
If if you had the wisdom of the
instructors who came before you

00:09:53.747 --> 00:09:55.830
and yes, you are teaching
with another instructor,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.950
they may know this,
but they may not.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:04.500
You would know that you have
to add a select command.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.520
And so what we do is we allow
instructors to communicate

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.730
through time and
space by including

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.520
by allowing them to include
instructor notes directly

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.870
into the lessons in the
context that is needed.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.600
So that when you
teach this lesson,

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.290
you can change your behavior
and you can show the learners

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.080
what exactly they need
to know and reduce

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.160
their cognitive load.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.710
And another way we
champion people first

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:35.730
is that we include automation.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:40.990
Oftentimes as you're building,
R Markdown, packages will update.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:42.160
Things fail.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:43.360
You get a lot of errors.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.420
You get warnings
that don't make sense

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:46.890
because the package
authors change

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.190
their change their packages.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:52.990
We avoid this by embedding renv.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.440
renv sorry.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.940
In on the back end of sandpaper.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:00.360
And the beauty about this.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.220
You don't need to know anything
about renv in order to use this.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.120
We lock the packages, we give
you a way to update them,

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.440
and we give you a way to pin
them so that your lessons are

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.080
always working
and you have a way

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:18.390
to investigate what happens
when packages update.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.930
And I'm going to show you
that some of that a little bit

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:22.370
later.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:25.490
And really, this
leads me into the fact

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:28.650
that we championed
access for all.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.200
Because it shouldn't
matter what packages

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:32.530
you have on your system.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.230
It shouldn't matter
what packages you

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.490
have in your global library.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.110
You should be able to
build the lesson the same.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.020
Anyone else can
build the lesson.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.070
And so.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:45.400
I'm going to show
you, like what happens

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.650
when you don't have your own
computer, when you're not

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.470
on your own computer and
you receive a pull request

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:53.470
that changes a plot.

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:55.420
But can anybody
tell me what's going

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.440
to happen when this happens?

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.890
Probably not because
it's R code,

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:05.310
but seeing the output of
the plot is night and day.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:09.420
And we and we have embedded
pull request previews

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:14.530
for the intermediate
Markdown, for these lessons.

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:17.610
So that you can confirm
that things actually

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:21.260
work whether or not you
have the tools installed.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:24.610
And this is.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:28.480
And furthermore, if you're
writing an R lesson,

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:33.220
sometimes you may forget to
add, to include, alternative text

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:36.520
or we have link descriptions
that don't actually describe

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.620
anything and you say, whoops.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.180
And it's.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.990
It's this kind of.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.900
This kind of.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.790
Pre-flight validation
that's done

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:54.270
by pegboard that allows you
to take a step back and say,

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.850
yeah, I made a
mistake and fix it.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.910
And that's really
the key to, that's

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.670
really the key, to how we're
championing access for all.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:07.530
And we're always learning
because we're humans.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.090
You forget things.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:10.270
You shouldn't be punished.

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:13.110
You shouldn't have,
like, glaring errors

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:16.270
when you miss something.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.770
When you mistype something.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.080
And it shows that
we're always learning

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.290
and I'm going to turn on myself.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.970
I'm obviously.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.030
Sandpaper is not the
fastest thing in the world.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.160
The Workbench is not the
fastest thing in the world.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:37.860
But I really want to address the
pink pachyderm in the premises.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.270
We don't use Quarto yet.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:46.060
Sorry, but the way I design,
the way I design the system,

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.940
we're going to include
